A||N||10||1FST2M6||Anyone into Mountain biking?||||1||
Z||000000||1FST2M6||10-09-2000||05:00 PM||||We (Lyndsay girlfriend of 3 years and myself) just got back from looking at Mountain bikes.. anyone have good suggestions.. looking at a K2 EVO 3 (disc brake) and she wants a Specialized Rockhopper pro. both full suspension bikes. any  issues with the manufacturers listed? i've got a nice set of fitted K2 Rollerblades and K2 kenetic snow skis. but don't really know much about their bikes.. and what about specialized? Consumer reports ranks them high but does anyone have experiance with one.. the sales reps love them.. but they love the HARO, the GT, the Trek... ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||10-09-2000||10:09 PM||||I don't know much about the K2. Specialized is re-designing the '01 Rockhopper FSR, so it isn't so "different"(to put it mildly). They are going back to the rectangular tubing without the weird curves near the seat post tube as the '00 model had. The pro is a very good model(top model RockHopper), but around here anyway, that would put you into a lower model StumpJumper for about the same cash. StumpJumper uses round tubing, and the suspension might be a up a notch(haven't seen any '01 models yet). Specialized has always put out a good quality bike. You could get yourself a NICE Cannondale hardtail for that kind of cash though. Unless, full suspension is what you're after.<P>Edited because I didn't read all the previous messgae.<P>I wouldn't consider what CONsumer reports has to say, they are very often wrong, and we have had very dissatisfied customers when they go with what CR says. I don't know much about K2 bikes, but everything else I have bought by them has been good.<P>Trek also puts out a good Bike. We carried the Trek brand for 2 years, and dropped them this spring. Although Trek makes a good bike, they have terrible customer service when it comes to warranty work etc. We had over $20,000 worth of back warranty issues with Trek, and they would never even get around to dealing with the issue. We dropped them once the back of the shop was filled with warranty issue Treks totalling over $30,000. Never again will we carry them. We only carried the full suspension Treks for one season. They never sold, and Specialized makes a superior model in every way. We picked up the Cannondale line, and they have been the best by far. Customer service is excellent, warranty work/issues are taken care of VERY quickly(usually under a week, trek=3-4 months), build quality and finish is excellent. Price is higher, but C'dales are hand built/assembled/painted etc in PA, and are an excellent choice. Specialized is also very good, I would assume K2 is a good choice also.<P><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||N_Man||10-10-2000||02:19 AM||||Well Id have to say that pretty much all MAJOR bike companys have a good product<P>I would reccomend riding as many bikes as you can, and if you get the oppourtunity ride them on the trails.  Buy the one the 'feels' the best.<P>Also warranty issues do make or brake the product.  Id have to agree about TREK being poor at warranty,  my manager bought a trek 9.8, he broke it within about 2 months and trek accused him of kicking it.  (Ive been at my shop for 4 years and my managers been there for awhile longer, And they said "well he must have kicked or somthing because our frames dont brake")||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||10-10-2000||04:16 AM||||Major bike company's put out a good product, but it's the little guys who really do the kick ass work!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The type of riding you do is really going to dictate the type of bike you need.. do you cruise the lakes?  Bomb the ski-hills?  Actually climb up the hills after you bomb them?  Whatever you get, make sure it <B>fits</B>!  That's the single most important part of buying a bike.  A $100 huffy will be more comftrable than a $4000 boinger if it fits better..<P>Full suspension designs are getting so effecient now, that unless you're a hardcore racer, there's really no reason not to get a full suspension bike.  <P>It's cool to see some other bike guys on here.  I was a mechanic for 4 years until the shop closed (long story)...  I'm going back in the spring.  I race my Independent Fabrications Deluxe, and have a Kona U'Hu dually for fun.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||10-10-2000||08:39 AM||||how bout GT? i rode an I-drive 4.0 last night... I picked up a cannondale Jeckyll 700 and it feels about 10lbs lighter than the GT. uum hey "56" do you ship bikes? Lyndsay really didn't like the full suspension ride last night..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> heh.. sorry.. she did like the A1 comp FS (Specialized) the K2 i looked at and the GT are about $1200 the K2 has disc brakes the GT has cantalever (sp) the GT has a strange 4 link suspensions (like the back of a Cobra mustang), while the K2 has a single swing arm(like a motorcycle). Glad to hear Specialized is a decent mark. and i'll loose the Consumer reports stuff i have. thanks for all your help. I'd love a Cannondale but really want a full FS bike. (and the C'dales FS bakes are to much for me... I'd love to have a Raven though!) a few friends of mine are down hill screamers and the ones with fully suspended bikes seem to fall less than the hardtail guys. I like the IF bikes but theres noone around here for support if i trash it. Lyndsay just wants a nice bike. thanks for your help... any ideas about the GT?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-10-2000||11:33 AM||||We carry the GT brand, but only the BMX lineup. I don't have any experience with their MTN bikes, but they have always been considered a top notch bike. BMX bikes aren't bad, but Specialized again(IMO)makes a better BMX bike.<P>If you are talking about the Rockhopper comp, it is a great bike for the money. We have one left and I think it is going for $730. The base RH is going for $500 right now at our shop. A Rockhopper full suspension retailed for $1100 at our shop, and the base Cannondale super-v(can't remember model #)also goes for $1100. Cannondale has had a slight problem with the single swing-arm bikes, the swing arm snaps. Happend to an employee at our shop while he was riding down the street even. Cannondale is fixing the problem for '01.<P>I think our website is <A HREF="http://www.bikesourceonline.com," TARGET=_blank>http://www.bikesourceonline.com,</A>  not sure though, haven't checked it in over a year. <BR>We do ship, but not mail order. If you really want to go that route, e-mail me, and I can see what we can do.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||10-10-2000||11:43 AM||||SWEET! SUPER V for less than 2K!!! humm...  i'll bug ya in a few hours.. gotta talk to the girlfriend... the super v has provisions for disc brake upgrades right...  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||AkursedX||10-10-2000||05:00 PM||||Hmmm, Mountain Biking and Fiero's, those two just don't seem to fit together to me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||11
Z||000008||1FST2M6||10-10-2000||05:03 PM||||they'll hook on the chezy pseudo trailer hitch from the fiero store with the bike rack kit..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but they'll live more often on the back of the Saturn or the Alfa 164.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||10-10-2000||10:18 PM||||Its a pretty big and exensive undertaking to install disks on a bike not factory equipped. First the wheel sets need to be replaced, you need a hub that will accept a disk. Then you need the hydraulic setup, and all the disk hardware. Plus, you need a bike with the frame designed to mount the calipers to. The lower priced super-v has the frame mounts, but it would be pretty expensive to put a disk setup on. Plus, as you go higher in price, shocks become better with lock-outs, better drive-train, lighter frames, etc. I'll see if any of the guys at the shop have any bikes for sale at the moment, their bikes are always LOADED with tons of cool expensive hardware, that gets sold cheap because we get to E.P. everything.<P><BR>Here is Cannondales website. I don't know if pricing is on there or not: <A HREF="http://www.cannondale.com/bikes/usa/welcome.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cannondale.com/bikes/usa/welcome.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||10-11-2000||04:43 AM||||Unless you're doing alot of muddy/wet riding or rim-busting rides disc brakes aren't that big of an advantage.  I'm using Avid Arch Supremes and boy do they stop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I love employee purchasing!  <P>I haul my bike with my GT all the time!  Took it to a few races this summer when I wasn't going with anybody else.  I'm not worried about the paint so I just got a cheapo $15 trunk rack and it works great!  Actually very stable.  Thule makes a roof rack setup for the Fiero but I wasn't prepared to spend that kind of money at the time.. <P>The interesting thing is that my race bike almost costs more than <B>both</B> my fieros combined  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
